        <item>
            <title>1.2.6</title>
            <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 00:23:14 -0400</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>1.2.6</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>1.2.6</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>14.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <description sparkle:format="markdown"><![CDATA[### Recent files, one key away

Saved a PDF or downloaded something and need it in a terminal or an email?
Open Cubby and press **←**. The text list becomes the newest files in your
Downloads folder and on your Desktop, with your screenshots still on the right.

Use **↑ ↓** to pick one and **↩** or a digit to paste it. A terminal gets the
file's path, quoted so spaces work. Gmail, Mail, Slack and everything else get
the file itself. **→** takes you back to your text, and **⌘R** shows the file
in Finder.

It's off until you switch it on in **Settings → Files**. That's when macOS
asks to let Cubby see those two folders. Cubby only looks when you press ←,
keeps no record of your files, and never moves or deletes them.
]]></description>
